    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `check_server(server, cases)` in `mcpcheck/inproc.py` and added tests for it. `pytest -q` passes (4 tests).\n\n**How it works:** it uses the `Client` class that comes with the pinned `mcp==2.2.0`, which can connect to a server object directly in memory, with no subprocess or sockets. It runs `Client(server, mode=\"legacy\", cache=None)`, then calls `client.session.call_tool(...)` for each case and passes each result to the same `compare` function, keeping mismatches in case order. I used `mode=\"legacy\"` because it does the same handshake (`session.initialize()`) that `check_command` does over stdio. The default `\"auto\"` mode would take a different in-process path that skips the handshake. I also turned off the client's response cache so every case really reaches the server.\n\n**Tests:** `tests/test_inproc.py` builds a server with the SDK's decorator API (`MCPServer` plus `@server.tool()`). It checks that passing cases return no mismatches, and that failing cases come back in order with the server's actual output. There's no pytest-asyncio installed, so the tests use `anyio.run`, which is already installed with `mcp`.\n\n**Same results as stdio:** I ran one set of cases through both `check_command` (as a real subprocess) and `check_server`. The set included a mismatch, a passing case, an unknown tool, and invalid arguments. Both returned exactly the same mismatch lists, including the error text.\n\n**One cleanup left:** I couldn't delete the scratch script from that comparison because the `rm` needed your approval. Please delete `_parity_check.py` from the project root. It isn't in `tests/`, so pytest won't run it in the meantime.\n\nNo dependencies were added or changed."
  },
